 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Polarity",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Pin 1 is the anode (+), pin 2 the cathode. A green mark in one corner of the top face and a green triangle on the underside both point out the pin 2 end. On the carrier tape the cathode end lies toward the sprocket holes and the anode end away from them."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Drive",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "The LED needs a current-limiting resistor in its circuit and is run at its rated current; with no resistor, a small change in supply voltage swings the current widely and can burn it out. Surge voltages when the circuit is switched on or off destroy it. Too much forward or reverse voltage damages it; the reverse voltage across it stays at 5 V or less, lit or under test."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Current, temperature and colour",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Colour moves slightly with drive current, and a series resistor for each LED is the suggested arrangement. Its own heating and the ambient temperature change it as well: efficiency drops and colour shifts as it warms, so heat removal is a design concern. The allowed DC current is 25 mA up to 25 °C ambient and falls to about 6.7 mA at 85 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bins",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "LEDs from different bins used in one product can differ visibly in colour; use a single bin per product."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Handling and soldering",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The encapsulant is soft and the emitting face and plastic body are easily damaged by force, so handle the part gently. Reflow to the profile, at most twice, with an easily washed flux, and keep the emitting face clean, since residue changes the colour. Hand soldering is only for repair and rework: once, at no more than 300 °C, within 3 s, with an iron of 25 W or less. Do not touch the lens while hot; after soldering apply no force or heavy vibration to it, do not bend the board, and protect the part from impact."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Cleaning and chemicals",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Ultrasonic cleaning is not allowed. Isopropyl alcohol or pure alcohol may be used to wipe or soak it for up to 1 minute, followed by 15 minutes at room temperature before use. Thinners, trichloroethylene, acetone, sulphides, nitrides, acids, alkalis and salts damage the LED."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Potting and adhesives",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Volatile substances can enter the package and, with light and heat in operation, discolour it and cause heavy loss of output. Materials that harm the LED must not be used; compatibility tests of every material for the intended use are advised, and adhesives that give off volatile organic gas are not to be used when mounting. Before using a normal potting compound, a small sample lit for 168 h at room temperature is the suggested trial."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Storage and moisture",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Unopened, store at 30 °C or below and below 60 % RH, and use within one year. The LED is moisture sensitive: after opening, use within 7 days at 30 °C or below and within 60 % RH; absorbed moisture can crack the package in reflow and alter the colour. Unused parts are dried by baking, 60 ±5 °C for 24 h on the reel or 105 ±5 °C for 1 h loose, then sealed in a foil bag or kept in a nitrogen dry cabinet. Keep the storage free of acid, alkali and corrosive gas, strong vibration and strong magnetic fields."
  },
  {
   "topic": "ESD",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Static and voltage spikes damage the LED, including transients at turn-on and turn-off. Wrist straps, gloves and shoes that are anti-static, and properly grounded equipment, are recommended. A damaged LED shows clearly higher leakage, lower forward voltage at low current, or fails to light at low current."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Packing",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "3000 pieces per reel on 8 mm carrier tape. The labelled reel is packed in an anti-static, moisture-proof aluminium foil bag."
  }
 ],
